    "DifficultyScore": 3,
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under decision theory, expected monetary value (EMV) is the probability-weighted average of each project's payoffs — a statement about the CENTER of the outcome distribution. It says nothing about the SPREAD. A risk-averse manager has a concave utility function, so a guaranteed dollar is worth more than a lottery ticket with the same expected value; the utility loss from a bad outcome exceeds the utility gain from an equally likely good one. Such a manager may rationally prefer Project B's stable returns even though its EMV is slightly lower, or demand a risk premium to take Project A. Crescent's choice depends on risk tolerance, not on EMV alone. A common error is declaring the higher-EMV project optimal for everyone; EMV is a decision INPUT, and risk preference is a legitimate, rational part of the decision.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "EMV is an average, not a universal prescription. A risk-averse manager at Crescent may assign more utility weight to avoiding Project A's bad outcomes than to earning its good ones, and therefore rationally prefer Project B. The higher-EMV project is optimal only for a risk-NEUTRAL decision-maker, which is one preference among many.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Choosing the lower-EMV project can be perfectly rational when it carries lower variance and the manager is risk-averse. Crescent's decision-maker trades expected value against risk through a utility function — sacrificing a small amount of EMV to avoid large potential losses is standard, defensible behavior, not an error.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Variance matters whenever outcomes are uncertain and decision-makers are risk-averse. Two projects at Crescent can share an EMV while one is far riskier; ignoring that spread would treat a certain $100,000 and a coin flip between $0 and $200,000 as identical, which no risk-averse manager would do.",
