    "ExplanationCorrect": "The indifference point is the volume at which the two cost structures have equal total cost: $100,000 + $8Q = $40,000 + $12Q. Rearranging: $60,000 = $4Q → Q = 15,000 units. Below 15,000 units, Option 2 (low fixed, high variable) is cheaper; above 15,000 units, Option 1's lower variable cost dominates. Ironwood's CFO can use this to pick the structure based on expected volume relative to 15,000 units. The general formula: indifference point = difference in fixed costs ÷ difference in variable cost per unit = ($100,000 − $40,000) ÷ ($12 − $8) = 15,000. A common error is summing the fixed costs or dividing the fixed-cost difference by only one variable cost instead of their difference.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "10,000 units comes from summing BOTH fixed costs and dividing by the average variable cost: ($100,000 + $40,000) ÷ 14 = 10,000, where 14 is the average of $8 and $12. The indifference condition compares the TOTAL cost of each option at the same volume — it requires their difference ($60,000 and $4), not their sum.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "5,000 units results from dividing the fixed-cost difference by the HIGHER variable cost alone: ($100,000 − $40,000) ÷ $12 = 5,000. The correct denominator is the DIFFERENCE between the variable costs ($12 − $8 = $4), because that is the rate at which the two total-cost lines converge as volume rises.",
