    "ExplanationCorrect": "With two binding constraints, the optimum lies at a corner point of the feasible region, found by solving the constraint equations simultaneously. Corners are: (0,0) $0, (800,0) = 800 P x $50 = $40,000 with labor 1,600 feasible, (0,600)= $24,000, and intersection of 3P+2Q=2,400 and 2P+3Q=1,800. Solving: Q=(2,400-3P)/2 into second => 2P+3*(2,400-3P)/2=1,800 => 4P+7,200-9P=3,600 => P=720, Q=120. Contribution at intersection = 720x$50 + 120x$40 = $36,000+$4,800=$40,800, the highest feasible. Business interpretation: under two constraints single-resource ranking fails; corner evaluation prevents ranking errors. Common trap is choosing the 800P single-constraint optimum or the 0/600 labor-only optimum or an even mix.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "480 units of P and 320 units of Q yields $37,600 but is not the feasible intersection of 3P+2Q=2,400 and 2P+3Q=1,800; the true intersection is at 720 P and 120 Q. A candidate selecting this option has solved the simultaneous equations incorrectly or used different constraint coefficients. Total contribution at 480/320 is lower than at the true corner 720/120.",
