    "ExplanationCorrect": "In a make-or-buy analysis, the indifference volume is the annual quantity at which total cost to make equals total cost to buy. Only avoidable costs are relevant; allocated corporate overhead is not avoidable and should be excluded. Relevant make cost = variable cost ($8/unit) + avoidable fixed cost ($90,000 equipment depreciated over 5 years gives $18,000/year). Buy cost = $14 per unit x quantity. Annual indifference: $18,000 + $8Q = $14Q, so $18,000 = $6Q, giving Q = 3,000 units/year. Cumulative over 5-year equipment life: total units needed = $90,000 / $6 = 15,000 units total, or 15,000/5 = 3,000 units per year. Recomputed: indifference at 15,000 cumulative units (3,000/year for 5 years); A correctly identifies 15,000 units as the indifferent cumulative volume. Business interpretation: at 20,000 units annual demand, making in-house saves $6 x 20,000 - $18,000 = $102,000 per year. A common trap is to include allocated overhead, which inflates the indifference volume and could lead to a buy decision.",
