    "ExplanationWrongB": "$200 results from setting P = MC + some margin, or from computing the midpoint incorrectly. If a candidate sets MR = 0 (revenue maximization instead of profit maximization): 500 - Q = 0, Q = 500, P = 500 - 0.5(500) = $250 - that is answer D. $200 may result from using the small business segment's demand function incorrectly.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "$400 results from confusing the quantity with the price. The profit-maximizing quantity is 400 hours, but the price is $300. A candidate selecting this answer has computed Q correctly but reported it as P. Alternatively, $400 may result from an incorrect application of the demand function.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "$250 is the revenue-maximizing price (where MR = 0), not the profit-maximizing price. At MR = 0: 500 - Q = 0, Q = 500, P = 500 - 0.5(500) = $250. Revenue maximization ignores the marginal cost of $100 per hour. Profit maximization requires MR = MC, which yields a higher price ($300) and lower quantity (400).",
    "FormulaReference": "DA-10: Profit maximization - Set MR = MC for each segment independently",
